contents.reasoningContent:
The user is asking a specific question about the memory flush tool restriction mechanism. They want to know:

1. Is the restriction applied at the execution level (i.e., the model sees all tools but gets blocked at runtime)?
2. Or is the restricted tool list already filtered before being sent to the AI (i.e., the model only sees the allowed tools in the tools definition)?

Let me look at the code to determine this. From what I've read:

In `agent-tools.ts`:

```typescript
const toolsForMemoryFlush: AnyAgentTool[] = isMemoryFlushRun && memoryFlushWritePath
  ? []
  : tools;

if (isMemoryFlushRun && memoryFlushWritePath) {
    for (const tool of tools) {
      if (!MEMORY_FLUSH_ALLOWED_TOOL_NAMES.has(tool.name)) {
        continue;  // ← 直接跳过，不加到 toolsForMemoryFlush
      }
      if (tool.name === "write") {
        toolsForMemoryFlush.push(
          wrapToolMemoryFlushAppendOnlyWrite(tool, { ... }),
        );
        continue;
      }
      toolsForMemoryFlush.push(tool);
    }
  }
```

This shows that the filtering happens BEFORE the tools list is sent to the model. Tools not in `MEMORY_FLUSH_ALLOWED_TOOL_NAMES` are skipped (continue), they're never added to `toolsForMemoryFlush`. And the write tool is replaced with a wrapped version.

Then `toolsForMemoryFlush` goes through the rest of the pipeline:
```typescript
const toolsForMessageProvider = filterToolsByMessageProvider(toolsForMemoryFlush, ...);
const toolsForModelProvider = applyModelProviderToolPolicy(toolsForMessageProvider, ...);
const subagentFiltered = applyToolPolicyPipeline({ tools: toolsForModelProvider, ... });
```

And final tools are what the model receives in its system prompt.

So the answer is clear: **the restriction is applied at the tools definition level, not the execution level**. The model never sees exec, bash, edit, etc. in the memory flush mode. It only sees `read` and a wrapped `write`.

Let me verify by also checking the `unavailableCoreToolReason` which is used in the system prompt to tell the model why other tools aren't available - this also confirms the tools are filtered at definition level because the model gets a message explaining the restricted toolset.

