Now I understand the full picture. The `unavailableCoreToolReason` is only used in **warning messages** when someone's tool policy contains unknown entries that don't match the current filtered tool list. It's a diagnostic aid, not a tool definition mechanism.

The actual filtering happens at the definition level in `agent-tools.ts` - the code I read at lines 1079-1110:

```typescript
if (isMemoryFlushRun && memoryFlushWritePath) {
    for (const tool of tools) {
      // 非 read/write 的 tool → continue（连 toolsForMemoryFlush 都不加进去）
      if (!MEMORY_FLUSH_ALLOWED_TOOL_NAMES.has(tool.name)) {
        continue;
      }
      // write 工具被替换为 append-only 版本
      if (tool.name === "write") {
        toolsForMemoryFlush.push(wrapToolMemoryFlushAppendOnlyWrite(tool, { ... }));
        continue;
      }
      // read 直接保留
      toolsForMemoryFlush.push(tool);
    }
  }
```

Then `toolsForMemoryFlush` (which only contains read and wrapped write) flows through the rest of the tool pipeline (provider policy, model policy, tool policy pipeline), but these only filter DOWN from what's already there. They can't add back tools that were already excluded.

So the answer is: **tools definition 层就已经筛选了。** The model never sees exec, bash, edit, etc. in its tool definitions. The execution layer doesn't need to block them because they were never offered.

